- Retrieved practices:
- Pass correct types to variadic functions using va_start
WHAT: Passing an argument of the wrong type (for example, std::string instead of const char *) to a variadic function that uses va_start/va_arg causes undefined behavior when the arguments are read.
WHY: Undefined behavior can lead to memory corruption, crashes, or exploitable vulnerabilities due to misinterpreted stack/argument data.
HOW: Ensure the last named parameter in va_start and all variadic arguments have exactly the types expected by va_arg; avoid passing non-POD or implementation-defined types through variadic ellipses.
EXAMPLE: Before (noncompliant, C++): void f(std::string s, ...); va_start(list, s); std::cout << va_arg(list, int); After (compliant): void f(const char *s, ...); va_start(list, s); std::cout << va_arg(list, int);

- Correct use of va_start in C++ variadic functions
WHAT: Misusing the second argument to va_start (e.g., passing a reference, array type, or a type incompatible with default argument promotions) in C++ variadic functions causes undefined behavior.
WHY: Undefined behavior can corrupt the stack, leak or overwrite sensitive data, or allow code execution vulnerabilities when handling variable arguments.
HOW: In C++ variadic functions with C linkage, ensure the va_start second argument is the identifier of the rightmost non-variadic parameter, is not a reference or array type, and is a trivially copyable, trivially destructible type compatible with default argument promotions.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `void f(int &x, ...){ va_list ap; va_start(ap, x); ... }` After (safer): `void f(int x, ...){ va_list ap; va_start(ap, x); ... }`

- Detect and handle memory allocation errors
WHAT: Failing to check the return value of memory allocation functions (for NULL) and then using the pointer or an offset from it, leading to invalid memory access.
WHY: Using a NULL or invalid pointer can cause crashes or enable exploits that write to unintended memory locations, compromising integrity and control flow.
HOW: After every dynamic allocation (malloc/calloc/realloc/new), immediately check for failure (NULL or exception) and abort, clean up, or safely degrade before any dereference or pointer arithmetic.
EXAMPLE (C++): Before: `int *p = (int*)calloc(n, sizeof(int)); p[0] = 1;` After: `int *p = (int*)calloc(n, sizeof(int)); if (!p) { handle_error(); return; } p[0] = 1;`

- Handle memory allocation failures in C++ code
WHAT: Failing to detect and handle memory allocation errors (null pointer from `new(std::nothrow)` or thrown `std::bad_alloc`) can lead to dereferencing invalid pointers and undefined behavior.
WHY: Using uninitialized or null pointers can cause crashes, data corruption, or exploitable memory-safety vulnerabilities.
HOW: After `new(std::nothrow)`, always check the returned pointer for `nullptr` before use and handle the error path; if using throwing `new`, wrap allocations in a `try`/`catch(std::bad_alloc&)` block and handle failures before using the pointer.
EXAMPLE: Before (unsafe): `int *p = new(std::nothrow) int[n]; std::memcpy(p, src, ...);` After (safe): `int *p = new(std::nothrow) int[n]; if (!p) { /* handle error */ return; } std::memcpy(p, src, ...);`

- Do not leak resources when handling exceptions
WHAT: Failing to release dynamically allocated resources when an exception is thrown (for example, returning or rethrowing before delete/free) causes resource leaks.
WHY: Resource leaks can exhaust memory or other resources, leading to denial of service, instability, or degraded performance.
HOW: Use RAII (resource acquisition is initialization) objects (e.g., smart pointers, wrapper classes) so that destructors automatically release resources in all control paths, including exceptions; avoid raw new/delete in code that can throw.
EXAMPLE: Before (leaks on exception): `T* p = new T(); risky(); delete p;` After (no leak): `std::unique_ptr<T> p = std::make_unique<T>(); risky();`

- Avoid resource leaks when handling exceptions in C++
WHAT: Failing to reclaim dynamically allocated resources when exceptions are thrown (including during construction) causes memory or handle leaks and partially initialized objects.
WHY: Leaked resources can exhaust memory or system handles, degrade availability, and leave objects in unsafe, inconsistent states.
HOW: Use RAII so resources are owned by stack objects whose destructors free them; avoid raw new/delete in user code; ensure constructors either fully initialize all RAII members or throw without leaking; never rely on manual cleanup paths that can be bypassed by exceptions.
EXAMPLE: Before (leaks on exception): `T* p = new T(); risky(); delete p;` After: `std::unique_ptr<T> p(new T()); risky();` (destructor frees `p` automatically on exception).



